{
  "result": "I've always loved the hours that don't belong to anyone. Between four and six in the morning, the world is not yet demanding anything from you. The emails haven't started. The traffic is still asleep. The sky is in that indecisive blue-gray state, not quite night, not quite day, as if it's holding its breath.\n\nI used to think being productive meant waking up and immediately attacking a to-do list. Now I think productivity is a poor substitute for presence. In those early hours, I don't try to optimize myself. I make coffee slowly, not because I'm performing a ritual for social media, but because the kettle actually takes that long to boil and the grounds bloom when you pour water over them. I watch it happen.\n\nThere's a strange kind of honesty in the pre-dawn quiet. Your mind hasn't yet put on its armor for the day. The thoughts that surface are less polished, more true. The worries are still there, but they haven't rehearsed their speeches yet. The hopes are quieter, but more sincere.\n\nMaybe that's why I write then. Not to produce something, but to listen. To catch what my brain says before the rest of the world tells it what to think. Most of it is forgettable. A line about the streetlight, a half-formed idea. But sometimes, a single clear sentence surfaces, and it feels like finding a coin in the sand. It was there all along. I just had to be quiet enough to notice it.",
